          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies the Sabbath centrally with sacred rest: creation rest, present salvation-rest in Christ, and final eschatological rest. Disputes mainly concern its continuing calendrical application.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies the Sabbath centrally as a complete seven-unit crowned by rest: a week, seventh-year cycle, or sevenfold cycle leading to Jubilee. Rest is the completing member, not the whole symbolic referent.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Sabbath signifies the seventh-day rest itself and, typologically, spiritual and eschatological rest in Christ.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Sabbath signifies the complete seven-count or unit whose final member is rest.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book reclassifies weekly Sabbath, land Sabbath, counted “sabbaths,” and related Greek expressions as instances of complete seven-units rather than primarily instances of rest. It consequently displaces the consensus’s Christological and eschatological rest identification from the core, although rest remains an essential completing element.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The book explicitly denies that the Sabbath’s primary identity is the seventh-day rest itself, treating that rest as one member that completes the larger seven. Thus the consensus and book assign different central referents to the same term.\",\"rationale\":\"This is not merely a broader account of Sabbath rest. The book replaces rest as the identified symbol with the complete seven-unit, while retaining rest only as that unit’s crowning member. No consensus alternative articulates this as the primary identification; however, because the book expressly rejects and replaces the established rest referent, DIVERGENT is more appropriate than NOVEL.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Leviticus 23 and 25 show that sabbath terminology can structure weeks and year-cycles, but they do not by themselves establish that “complete seven” is the term’s primary meaning rather than a cycle defined by recurring seventh-part rest. The English excerpts from Luke 18 and Matthew 28 also do not independently demonstrate the asserted Greek usage, while Hebrews 4:9 more directly supports the traditional rest identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
